{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC status — AC#3's drift protection is DELEGATED, not satisfied here\n\nRecording acceptance-criteria status explicitly, and flagging one that this change does **not**\nmeet.\n\n### AC#3 stays UNTICKED\n> \"Regression coverage pinning whichever behaviour is chosen, so the CLI surface cannot drift\n> silently.\"\n\n**What this change actually covers:** regression tests pinning **six measured commands** —\n`show`, `list`, `ready`, `sign`, `verify-signature`, `audit show`, plus `review-plan --status`\nand the `--output llm` projections. That is genuine coverage of those commands, including an\nexact-round-trip oracle that a float64-lossy implementation fails.\n\n**What it does not cover:** the clause \"so the CLI surface **cannot drift silently**\". A CLI\ncommand added *later* that emits a bare 19-digit `time.time_ns()` integer passes today by never\nbeing looked at — the never-ran class. The suite would be green because the new surface is\noutside its enumeration, not because the new surface is correct.\n\nSo AC#3 is **not** met by what shipped, and I am deliberately **not** rewording it to fit. The\ndrift protection is delegated to a new bug:\n\n- **`spherelike-oily-honeyeater` (`83eb-c3e1-3dfd-472f`)** — `scripts/check_cli_json_js_safe.py`\n  wired into `make lint`, linked `discovered_from` this ticket. Its acceptance criteria are\n  written to be non-vacuous: the load-bearing one requires the guard to **FLAG a\n  deliberately-seeded new CLI command** emitting a bare out-of-range integer, and to **PASS the\n  clean tree**. A guard that only ever passes has proven nothing. It also requires the in-scope\n  command set to be derived **structurally** from the CLI dispatch/parser registry rather than a\n  hand-maintained list inside the guard — a literal list reproduces the same drift problem one\n  level up.\n\n**Why it was split rather than added here.** This change is already at 160 non-test lines against\nthe Gerrit `bugfix-size-attestation` floor of 150. Adding a guard plus its tests would re-trigger\nthat block, and — because it would widen this ticket's recorded `file_impact` — it would **stale\nthe plan-review attestation** that the gate consumes, turning one problem into a larger one.\n\n### AC#1 and AC#2 are met by this change\n- AC#1 (decide and record the policy): Option A adopted; recorded in `docs/release-notes.md`,\n  `CHANGELOG.md`, and `docs/output-schemas.md`.\n- AC#2 (schemas + api-stability updated together, called out as a consumer-visible break): no\n  schema change was needed — the schemas already type these fields `[\"integer\",\"string\",…]` and\n  already state the WIRE FORM with no surface qualifier, so the prose became true rather than\n  changing. `docs/api-stability.md` already carries the rule and the worked example. The break is\n  recorded as BREAKING in both changelogs.\n\n### Deliberately NOT ticking the boxes in the description\nThe checkboxes are left as-authored. The plan-review attestation's signed material **pins this\nticket's description** (`material-part: description df194b5ff52c68f0 4423`), so editing the\ndescription — even only to tick a box — would stale the attestation and re-trigger\n`bugfix-size-attestation` on the open change. Status is therefore recorded in this comment\nrather than by mutating the plan. The boxes should be reconciled after the change lands.\n\n### Correction to an earlier attribution\nMy previous comment said the plan-review attestation was signed by \"a session or the operator on\nthis host\". That overstates the evidence. The DSSE principal\n`b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126` identifies the **signing environment**, not the actor, and\nevery session on this host shares it. The accurate statement: the attestation was **signed in\nthis environment** at 2026-08-28 22:57:09 UTC. I did not run the review, and who did is not\ndeterminable from the principal."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787959186410602740,"uuid":"2c2301dd-bd8b-4a21-b2b4-2e9ecb87eb9f"}